Docker Networking – Quick Cheat-Sheet

Purpose: Docker networking enables container-to-container, container-to-host, and external communication using isolated network stacks built on Linux namespaces and bridges.

Network Types

Bridge (default): Single-host communication; default bridge has no DNS, custom bridge supports DNS via container names (most commonly used).

Host: Container shares host’s network stack; no port mapping; high performance but low isolation.

None: No network access; maximum isolation.

Overlay: Multi-host networking; used with Docker Swarm / Kubernetes.

Macvlan: Container gets IP from physical LAN; appears as a real device.

Key Commands

docker network ls → list networks

docker network inspect <net> → view network details

docker network create <net> → create custom network

docker network connect <net> <container> → attach container

Container Communication

❌ Do NOT use container IPs (they change)

✅ Use container names / service names (Docker DNS)

DNS works only in user-defined networks, not default bridge

Port Mapping

Syntax: -p host_port:container_port

Example: -p 8080:80

Required for external access, not needed for internal container-to-container traffic

Docker DNS

Built-in DNS server

Resolves container names to IPs

Works automatically in custom bridge & Docker Compose networks

Docker Compose Networking

One network created per project by default

Services communicate using service names

No need to expose internal ports

Best Practices

Use custom bridge networks

Expose only required ports

Use service/container names, not IPs

Avoid host network in production unless required

Use overlay networks for multi-host setups

Common Interview Points

Default network → bridge

DNS works → user-defined networks

External traffic → port mapping

Internal traffic → no port mapping

Multi-host → overlay

Highest isolation → none network

Troubleshooting Tips

App not accessible → check port mapping & listen address (0.0.0.0)

Containers not talking → same network? DNS name correct?

No internet → DNS/network config issue
